<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- The complication's half of the watch's container. It only ever READS:
         the snapshot on this device is written by `WatchLinkClient` as each
         application context arrives, and a complication that wrote would be a
         second author of the one file every surface on the wrist trusts.

         Naming a group does not grant one. An App Group is granted by this
         entitlement and by nothing else, and an extension inherits nothing from
         the app it is embedded in — the watch app's entitlement does not cover
         the .appex inside it, any more than the phone app's covers
         FarCoolerActivity. Without this key
         `FileManager.containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)`
         returns nil, `SnapshotStore.read()` answers "nothing known" exactly as
         it does when no context has ever arrived, and the complication is
         permanently empty against a watch that is working fine.

         The identifier is `$(FARCOOLER_APP_GROUP)` — the same expression the
         watch app's entitlement and both Info.plists expand — and not anything
         derived from this target's own bundle identifier. This extension's
         identifier is the watch app's with `.widgets` appended and the watch
         app's is the phone app's with `.watchkitapp` appended, so
         `$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)` here would name a group nothing on this
         watch has ever written to.

         No `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)`: application-groups values are not
         team-prefixed the way keychain groups are. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(FARCOOLER_APP_GROUP)</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
